From: Steam-Link-PS5
Subject: video: only request a keyframe on a forward sequence gap, drop resent frames

The host re-sends old video frames (observed on a wired-PC link: ~43% of
received packets were duplicates and frame sequences arrived thousands out of
order, jumping forwards then backwards). The sequence check treated every
mismatch -- including these old resent frames -- as a lost frame and requested
a keyframe, and each keyframe request made the host re-send still more old
frames, so the client thrashed and sat in "wait for keyframe" for ~20s at a
time while audio kept playing. Compare the 16-bit sequence as signed: a
backward difference is a resent duplicate, dropped silently without disturbing
our tracking or asking for anything; only a forward gap (real loss) requests a
keyframe. This breaks the re-send storm that caused the stalls.

diff -ru a/src/session/channels/video/ch_data_video.c b/src/session/channels/video/ch_data_video.c
--- a/src/session/channels/video/ch_data_video.c	2026-10-09 16:21:47.121356181 -0700
+++ b/src/session/channels/video/ch_data_video.c	2026-10-09 16:21:47.139340322 -0700
@@ -236,8 +236,19 @@
             IHS_SessionChannelDataLost(channel);
             videoCh->states.waitingKeyFrame = IHS_TimerNow();
         }
-    } else if (vhead.sequence != videoCh->states.expectedSequence) {
-        if (videoCh->states.waitingKeyFrame == 0) {
+    } else {
+        /* Only a FORWARD jump means frames were actually lost. A backward sequence is
+         * an old frame the host re-sent (a duplicate); the original code treated it as
+         * loss and asked for a keyframe, and each request made the host re-send more old
+         * frames, so the sequence thrashed forwards and backwards and the client sat in
+         * "wait for keyframe" for many seconds -> the long stalls. Drop resent/old frames
+         * silently without touching our sequence tracking, and only request a keyframe on
+         * a real forward gap. Sequence is a 16-bit wrapping counter, so compare signed. */
+        int16_t seqDiff = (int16_t) (vhead.sequence - videoCh->states.expectedSequence);
+        if (seqDiff < 0) {
+            goto unlock;
+        }
+        if (seqDiff > 0 && videoCh->states.waitingKeyFrame == 0) {
             IHS_SessionLog(channel->session, IHS_LogLevelWarn, "Video",
                            "Unexpected video frame sequence %u (expect %u), request keyframe", vhead.sequence,
                            videoCh->states.expectedSequence);
